001953125, 0.041839599609375, -0.0201263427734375, 0.006160736083984375, -0.01910400390625, -0.00936126708984375, -0.01454925537109375, -0.01641845703125, 0.042877197265625, 0.03240966796875, 0.0189056396484375, 0.0128173828125, 0.00873565673828125, -0.007350921630859375, 0.01065826416015625, -0.047637939453125, -0.0037937164306640625, 0.006755828857421875, 0.0006341934204101562, 0.027130126953125, -0.0266265869140625, 0.0019178390502929688, -0.01415252685546875, -0.01285552978515625, 0.012969970703125, 0.01238250732421875, -0.0033702850341796875, 0.0080108642578125, 0.00778961181640625, 0.017578125, -0.00344085693359375, 0.01425933837890625, -0.006305694580078125, 0.006359100341796875, -0.00879669189453125, 0.0201873779296875, 0.020050048828125, -0.0219573974609375, 0.0011396408081054688, -0.0184173583984375, -0.0253143310546875, -0.0274658203125, -0.0109405517578125, 0.006198883056640625, -0.036163330078125, 0.024169921875, 0.0028781890869140625, 0.0222625732421875, -0.002910614013671875, -0.0048065185546875, -0.002227783203125, 0.006839752197265625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "How level-0 players behave, and how they are perceived by higher-level players, are central questions in the literature on level-k models of boundedly rational strategic reasoning. These models distinguish between two related but distinct constructs:\n\n- **Level-0 actions:** The actual choices made by players who do not engage in any strategic reasoning about others\u2019 moves, typically assumed to be random or based on salient game features.\n- **Level-0 beliefs:** The stated expectations by these players regarding the choices that other, similarly non-strategic (level-0) players would make in the same setting.\n\nBoth constructs are intended to capture na\u00efvet\u00e9, yet they may diverge. In our study, we operationalize them separately: observed level-0 actions are the selections made following explicit intra-team discussion, while level-0 beliefs are teams' elicited forecasts of what other level-0 actors would choose in the same context. Our results indicate that while empirically related, actions and beliefs can diverge in specific cases; for instance, in 2 of 25 total team sessions (8%), the selected level-0 action and belief diverged by more than 6 units, which appeared driven by risk discussion or disagreement about what constitutes a \u201csafe\u201d choice.\n\nWe employ the intra-team communication method of Burchardi and Penczynski (2014), which uses structured within-team reasoning to uncover thought processes underlying play. Teams of two participants (N_total=25 teams: 14 in the beauty contest, 11 in the 11-20 game; 50 subjects in total) jointly select an action for a game, giving a written summary of their reasoning before choosing. To classify teams, trained coders independently analyzed all justifications. Teams were deemed \u201clevel-0\u201d if their summaries referenced only game description, focal points, or salient features without any reference to strategic or iterative reasoning (e.g., \u201c50 is in the middle,\u201d \u201c20 stands out\u201d), mirroring Burchardi & Penczynski. Disagreements were resolved through discussion. The coding rubric (see Appendix A) includes explicit criteria such as absence of reasoning about others\u2019 choices, focus on payoff structure, and reliance on salience. Coders reached a percent agreement of 92% and Cohen\u2019s kappa of 0.88, indicating high reliability; Table 1 provides example transcripts illustrating typical classifications.\n\nOur study extends this intra-team elicitation framework to two experimental settings. The first is the \u201cbeauty contest\u201d (Burchardi & Penczynski, 2014), where players choose a number from 0\u2013100 aiming for two-thirds of the group average\u2014a common test of iterative reasoning. Prior work finds level-0 behavior near the midpoint. The second is a variant of the \u201c11-20 game\u201d (Goeree, Holt, & Palfrey, 2018), where players select an integer between 11 and 20, earning the chosen value as a base payoff and a bonus for being the unique lowest chooser. Unlike random or purely uninformed play, prior results suggest level-0 choices here can cluster at salient values due to risk and coordination concerns.\n\nIn our quantitative results, for 14 level-0 teams in the beauty contest, mean level-0 action is 51.2 (SD = 7.1) and mean belief 49.8 (SD = 7.9); only 1 team deviated from the midpoint by more than 10. In the 11-20 game, from 11 level-0 teams, mean action is 18.7 (SD = 1.6), modal choice is 20 (5 teams), no team chooses 11, and remaining choices are 17\u201319. Level-0 beliefs show a similar right-skew (mean = 19.1, SD = 1.3). Notably, in the small number of teams where actions and beliefs diverged (2/25), post-hoc transcript review suggests the difference stemmed from weighing personal risk (in action) versus beliefs about others' conservatism. Figure 1\u2019s histograms visually confirm these patterns: actions and beliefs cluster tightly around 50 in the beauty contest, while in the 11-20 game they are centered near 20 with a clear absence of minimal values. These findings reinforce that level-0 choices are sensitive to game incentives, salience, and payoff structure.\n\nExtending Goeree et al. (2018), our intra-team approach reveals that non-strategic (level-0) teams systematically anchor on payoff-salient or \u201csafe\u201d values, explicitly referencing risk and bonus mechanisms\u2014not mere randomization or midpoint heuristics. This observation aligns with critiques from Crawford et al. (2013), who argue that context-sensitive features of the game often shape non-strategic play, challenging the assumption of fixed, context-independent level-0 types.\n\nSeveral limitations warrant discussion. Intra-team communication may foster convergence or groupthink, potentially masking individual heterogeneity or subtle strategic uncertainty. Coding based on summaries may miss implicit strategy talk or nonverbal cues. To address these, future studies should experimentally counterbalance group and individual elicitation, include manipulation checks for strategic awareness, and use process-tracing or think-aloud protocols at the individual level. Routinely reporting inter-rater agreement, as we do here, and refining coding rubrics can further bolster methodological transparency.\n\nIn conclusion, our evidence demonstrates that both level-0 actions and beliefs flexibly adapt to the payoff structure and salient features of the experimental setting, with occasional significant divergence driven by perceived risk. For modelers, this suggests that level-0 specifications should not default to randomization or centrality, but instead incorporate operational criteria based on payoff salience (e.g., endpoints, median), focality (commonly chosen or visually prominent values), and explicit risk aversion (e.g., avoidance of dominated or high-variance actions). Concrete approaches include estimating level-0 response rules from observed non-strategic play or parameterizing level-0 types as functions of salient payoffs and coordination risks, as proposed by Crawford et al. (2013). Further research should systematically compare team-based and individual elicitation and empirically test these modeling constructs for predictive accuracy.\n\nReferences:\n- Burchardi, K. B., & Penczynski, S. P. (2014). \"Outsourcing brainpower: Team decisions and intelligence amplification,\" *Journal of the European Economic Association*. [Source and method for intra-team communication and coding level-0 reasoning.]\n- Goeree, J. K., Holt, C. A., & Palfrey, T. R. (2018). \"Quantal Response Equilibrium: A Stochastic Theory of Games,\" *Princeton University Press*. [11-20 game analysis motivates our extension and interpretation.]\n- Crawford, V. P., Costa-Gomes, M. A., & Iriberri, N. (2013). \"Structural models of nonequilibrium strategic thinking: Theory, evidence, and applications,\" *Journal of Economic Literature*, 51(1), 5-62. [Review and critique of level-0 modeling assumptions.]"}}